we recently took a look at razors latest  14 inch gaming laptop the new blade  which we appreciated for its sturdy  design and solid performance we also  fell in love with its high res screen a  qhd+ panel with a native resolution of  3200 by 1800 but despite looking great  its admittedly over the top in a major  contributor to the increase in price  over the previous 14 inch blade razors  17 inch blade Pro is also back for 2014  and while it hasn't been tweaked as much  as its smaller cousin it's definitely an  improvement over the last year at a  glance the new blade pro mirrors the  physical design of last year's model  sharing the same dimensions and ports  with the only difference being that it's  0.04 pounds lighter the thin aluminum  chassis feels great and for a 17-inch  gaming laptop the blade pro is as thin  and light as it currently guess though  it's great to see that some things never  change  including the blades fantastic keyboard  the switchblade UI trackpad is a feature  that's needed refining since its  inception and unfortunately nothing's  changed since last year  the promise of the switchblade UI a  full-color touchscreen display with 10  customizable keys with displays of their  own isn't as enticing as it's made out  to be with the secondary display you can  run apps alongside or in unison with  desktop apps and with the customizable  keys you can define hotkeys for various  applications and implement full-color  icons to give them character sure enough  these features work but the problem is  the compromises that arise from their  inclusion especially the touchscreen the  issues with the Switchblade UI go beyond  its distinct features for one the  plastic surface that protects the  display is a poor surface for a laptop  trackpad but simply the surface tension  is easily disrupted by even the  slightest amount of residue resulting in  an input that feels inconsistent it's  positioned to the right of the keyboard  which is unusual for a laptop and will  take some getting used to on the plus  side it makes sense from a comfort  standpoint if you can't use a mouse when  gaming on the go the 10 customizable  keys are functionally better than the  trackpad but despite razors marketing  images the perky displays are recessed  into the keys  rather than positioned on the top and  the lensing effect that's used to direct  the image towards the left done for  visibility makes things a blurry mess  from any other angle personally I found  no worth in the ability to run apps on  my keyboard if I'm playing a game I'm  focused on the action on screen and if I  have the time to shift my attention away  from the game  focused on the Switchblade screen and I  could just as easily alt-tab into  another window on my actual monitor the  new pro model also comes with the same  CPU as last year and intel core i7 4708  q quad-core CPU at 2.4 gigahertz but  it's paired with a new GPU Nvidia is  860m in addition to 16 gigabytes of ddr3  RAM which is twice as much as the  previous model the 860m represents a  significant jump in performance over  last year's 765 in but it's not as  strong as the 870 in the 14 inch blade  in terms of hardware the blade pros  advantage is that it has a slightly  faster CPU and twice as much RAM but  when it comes to in-game performance the  14 inch blade is the stronger contender  the blade Pro does benefit from slightly  better battery life lasting around 90  minutes during extreme gaming scenarios  and close to five hours while web  browsing word processing and media  streaming sessions the 1080p LCD panel  on the blade pro is neither impressive  nor disappointing given its size and the  money spent on other features it's a tad  unfortunate that it maxes out at 1920 by  1080 but it doesn't stand out in  practice only when you're weighing the  merits of the screen relative to the  rest of the laptops premium features in  terms of inputs the blade pro has the  essentials cover three USB 3.0 ports  Ethernet headphone and microphone combo  port and HDMI out it's worth noting that  it lacks an SD card slot which is  quickly becoming commonplace in the  laptop market considering that razer  sells this laptop for a minimum of 2,300  dollars for the 128 gigabyte model it's  a shame that it hasn't kept up with  industry standards if you need more  storage prepared to spend $2,500 for the  256 gigabyte version or $2,800 for the  512 gigabyte model given that high cost  it's also disappointing that you'll have  to settle for medium to high graphics  settings very high or ultra settings are  simply out of reach if you want playable  framerates  running the same benchmarks as the blade  it's clear that the pro model is the  weakest of the two even though it's the  most expensive recommending the blade  pro in a world where the new blade  offers better performance with less  money is difficult the extra RAM and  faster CPU look good on paper but the  results speak for themselves  unless you need a 17 inch display you're  better off in almost every respect by  choosing the 14 inch blade if you want  one of razor's thin and light gaming  laptops it's better at gaming it  features a remarkable screen it's more  portable and it isn't plagued by a  trackpad that's occasionally  inconsistent
 

